e selected `/workspace/design-output/<project>/` tree, with `FANNAN_PROJECT` naming that project. The launcher sanitizes its own environment even when invoked directly; API keys and user-controlled command lookup do not flow into the executable. That binary file is **not supplied in the review pack**. A missing/wrong binary blocks the full checker. Do not use an unreviewed platform binary, re-pin a local fake or count a test stub as a production pass. The read-only precheck requires exactly path and project, confines writes to `/workspace/design-output/<project>/` and rejects repository, bare-repository, traversal, wrong-project, hard-link and symlink hazards as implemented. Every workflow calls it; evidence writers must not overwrite a previous version. The safe-write implementation uses Python 3 standard-library descriptor-relative opens, rejects symlinks, verifies directory ownership/modes and creates the leaf exclusively. It does not re-resolve the destination for a post-failure delete. This closes the path-replacement/symlink check-then-write flaw for untrusted users excluded by the checked permissions; it is not a host sandbox or protection from a concurrent process sharing the writer's identity or elevated privileges. The installer must provision `/workspace` and `/workspace/design-output` as real, root-owned directories without group/other write, and the existing project directory as owned by the dedicated non-root writer, also without group/other write. Every output subdirectory must meet that writer ownership/mode rule. Deny hostile same-identity writers on the actual host. If writing fails after exclusive creation, a partial new file can remain; block handoff and have its owner inspect/archive it before retrying. The helper never deletes a pathname after failure. Installation needs actual allowed-tools and write-denial evidence. No source code repository is writable by design. Keep encrypted backups using the project's approved storage and archive policy; the existing archive owner retains its separate retention authority. Do not copy secret values into review records. Fannan never uses an archive password. Log storage remains outside the code host. Existing routines stay deleted; recovering their missing verbatim archive requires the original file, not invented prompts. ## Runtime and dependencies The tool set is unchanged: Google DESIGN.md 0.4.0, axe-core 4.14.0, Playwright Core 1.64.0, Style Dictionary 5.6.1 and yaml 2.9.1, with the supplied lockfile. The implementation chooses the allowed Node **22.12+ reviewed-install route**. Node 20 is unsupported. The supplied r7 update and independent Grok report say Node v22.23.3 was already installed side by side at `/home/box/.local/node-v22.23.3` on Mi'mar's box with Fadel's yes. Do not reinstall or ask for that same decision again. This is reported host evidence, not fresh proof for a potentially different bot host; the package verification flags remain false. Keep the previous runtime for rollback; do not replace a shared global Node as an incidental design operation. Pin the exact reviewed runtime distribution/checksum in the host install record, check architecture and execute `node --version` on the actual selected binary. The concrete proposed distribution is **Node v22.23.3 Linux x64**, archive `node-v22.23.3-linux-x64.tar.xz`, SHA256 `df450af89261115ef9f9e3830c3eeb2cc9213b63c720b1af623cb5dcbe2e02de`. It was read from the [official versioned checksum list](https://nodejs.org/dist/v22.23.3/SHASUMS256.txt) on 16 October 2026 and is recorded in `runtime/node-runtime.json`. The checksum list was historically retrieved over HTTPS; its release signature and the archive bytes have not been verified in this R3 run. Grok reports a matching archive hash and attributes Amin's GPG verification as reported-only; the original Amin record is absent. This is an exact proposal, not an installed or Amin-approved runtime. Before approval, the system owner must verify Linux x64, libc compatibility and the official signed release checksum using trusted Node release keys; use a private versioned installation prefix and keep Node20 for rollback. A different architecture requires a newly reviewed artifact, never a quiet substitution. The writer also requires Python 3, GNU shell utilities and Linux x86_64/aarch64 with working `openat2` (Linux 5.6 or later, subject to host syscall policy); it introduces no pip dependency. It refuses unsupported hosts rather than falling back to pathname writes. [Linux openat2 semantics](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/openat2.2.html). For a reviewed staged installation, copy the candidate's package files to the external dependency prefix, then run: ```sh npm ci --ignore-scripts --prefix /opt/fannan/deps ``` Run using the reviewed Node binary's npm, not whichever npm is first on PATH. The dependency directory must be outside the manifest-covered release root; reject an in-tree prefix. Do not copy `node_modules` into the release or regenerate the lockfile to work around failures. Verify versions, the release lock hash and every installed dependency file against `runtime/dependency-trust.json` before any consumer imports code. The local staged inventory is candidate evidence, not Amin's approval. If target-platform installation differs, regenerate the inventory in an isolated staging review, update the release manifest and obtain review of that exact deployment; runtime must never auto-trust or auto-repin changes. Both bin and dependency prefixes and every ancestor must be real root-owned directories without group/other write; executable/dependency files are read-only to all writers except the trusted system owner (0555 executable, 0444 data). The verifier checks the complete dependency tree before imports, including lockfile ownership and modes, and requires exactly `/opt/fannan/deps`. Provisioning needs Fadel's decision and the system owner; no host provisioning was done here. A dedicated non-root runtime identity with no "... 9826 more characters
